# Auto-push to GitHub

After completing any code or config change in this repo, commit and push to GitHub without waiting for a separate request.

## Workflow

1. Finish the requested change and verify it works when practical.
2. Run `git status` and `git diff` to review what will be committed.
3. Stage only relevant files — never commit secrets (`.env`, credentials, `.streamlit/secrets.toml`).
4. Write a concise commit message focused on why the change was made.
5. Commit, then `git push origin HEAD` (or the current branch).
6. Confirm with `git status` that the working tree is clean and the push succeeded.

## Constraints

- Do not use destructive git commands (`push --force`, hard reset) unless explicitly asked.
- Do not skip hooks or amend commits unless the user rules for those cases apply.
- If there are no changes to commit, do not create an empty commit.
- If push fails (auth, conflicts), report the error and what the user needs to do.

## When to skip auto-push

- User explicitly says not to commit or push.
- Change is exploratory, incomplete, or the user asked for review only.
